      "text": "To find the distance between the foci (denoted as \\( 2c \\) in the ellipse, and denoted as \\( r \\) in the hyperbola), we can multiply the semi-latus rectum \\( l \\) by \\( 2 \\) to get \\( 2c \\). We know the formula \\( 2a = \\sqrt{l^2+4b^2} \\), where the semi-major axis is \\( a \\), the semi-minor axis is \\( b \\), and the semi-latus rectum is \\( l \\). \n\nGiven the foci distance in the ellipse:\n$$\ndistance\\_square = f^2 - b^2 + l^2\n$$\nTo find \\( c \\) ($$ 2c $$ in the ellipse), we use \\( a \\), \\( b \\), and \\( l \\):\n\\begin{align*}\n2a &= \\sqrt{l^2 + 4b^2} \\\\\na^2 &= \\frac{l^2 + 4b^2}{4}\n\\end{align*}\nThen,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2c &= a - b + \\frac{l}{2} \\\\\n    &= a + \\frac{l}{2} - b \\\\\n    &= \\left(\\frac{l^2 + 4b^2}{2}\\right) + \\frac{l}{2} - b \\\\\n    &= \\frac{l^2 + 5b^2}{2} \\\\\n    &= \\frac{1}{4} (l^2 + 4b^2) - 2b + \\frac{l}{2} - b \\\\\n    &= \\frac{1}{4} (l^2 + 4b^2) + \\frac{l}{2} - \\frac{5b}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo determine \\( l \\), we can set the distance (denoted as \\( r \\) in the hyperbola) equal to \\( 2c \\), which involves the following derived formula:\n$$\n2c = r\n$$\nThen,\n\\begin{align*}\nr^2 &= 2a^2 - b^2 + l^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{2(l^2 + 4b^2)}{4} - b^2 + l^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{l^2}{2} + b^2 + l^2 \\\\\n&= \\frac{l^2 + 2b^2}{2} + l^2\n\\end{align*}\nAs the given distances involve \\( l \\) and \\( b \\), the distance (denoted as \\( r \\)) between the foci is:\n$$\n\\boxed{4}\n$$",
