      "text": "We can rewrite the equation of the ellipse as follows: $\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1$.\n\nFrom the ellipse shown above in the diagram, we can see that the major axis of the ellipse has length 26. Since the major axis is the line passing through the center of the ellipse and both vertices, and the distance between the foci is related to the major axis, it means that the major axis of the ellipse is parallel to the imaginary axis, and the distance from the center to the vertices on the major axis is 13.\n\nUsing the formula for the distance from the center to a focus of an ellipse $\\sqrt{b^2 - a^2}$, we find that the distance from the center to the focus on the major axis is $\\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = 5$.\n\nUsing the diagram, we observe that the center of the ellipse is 1 unit above the bottom of the ellipse, so the distance between the foci is $2*$5=10.\n\nAssistant: That looks like the solution, but it is incomplete. The diagram says 2 vertices on the major axis. That means the distance spread out is 13 units. Consider the diagram below. The missing piece is the concentric circle, \n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.3 cm);\n\nint i, n = 10;\n\nfor (i = -n; i <= n; ++i) {\n  draw((i,-n)--(i,n),gray(0.7));\n  draw((-n,i)--(n,i),gray(0.7));\n}\n\ndraw((0,-n)--(0,n));\ndraw((-n,0)--(n,0));\n\ndraw(shift((1,1))*xscale(2)*yscale(6)*Circle((0,0),1),red);\n\ndraw(Circle((1,1),sqrt(52)));\n\ndot((1,1));\n[/asy] \n\nThis draws the ellipse and its major axis. The diagram now shows the major axis is a radius of the concentric circle. From here we should see that we can fit 6 rays from the center to the edges of the circle between the vertices of the ellipse. Since the ellipse major axis is 26, we need to find the sum of the lengths of all its rays. The reson we need the sum of the lengths is that we need to fill (26-25*2=3 sub segments which have 1 length. The length in question is the length of 4 rays which is $4*sqrt(52)$ The final answer is the sum of all the rays is $2*sqrt(52)$ or 10 plus 9 = $\\boxed{19}$.",
